<div id="2026-11-05-git-target-fail-closed" class="cl-entry">
<h3>2026-11-05 — Git dashboard: an unknown <code>?target=</code> fails closed (OpenBafangTool back)</h3>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>What.</strong> <code>/admin/git?target=openbafang</code> and its Hermes button silently served the local Comserv checkout and refused to start Hermes. The cause was data, not code: <code>root/config/worktrees.json</code> (gitignored, hand-edited) had been rewritten with <code>"external_repos": {}</code>, so <code>resolve_target('openbafang')</code> matched nothing. The entry is restored, and four changes stop a repeat from being silent:</p>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>1. Unknown targets fail closed.</strong> <code>Comserv::Util::Git::resolve_target</code> used to fall back to the local repo for an unregistered target — the dashboard then showed, and <code>op=commit</code> would have written to, the Comserv checkout under a URL naming another repo. It now returns <code>{ mode =&gt; 'unknown', repo =&gt; undef, error =&gt; ... }</code> and logs at ERROR (so the error audit sees it). <code>_run</code> refuses to execute any git command for a resolved target carrying an error, and <code>_git_list</code> returns <code>('', -1)</code> instead of falling through to the local repo (that path feeds <code>_validate_paths</code> for writes).</p>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>2. No hardcoded external rows.</strong> <code>root/admin/git/index.tt</code> carried a hardcoded OpenBafangTool dev-server row plus an "OpenBafangTool git" link, and <code>[% NEXT IF b.external %]</code> skipped the registry-driven external row — so the UI advertised a target the registry did not have. Both surfaces (<code>index.tt</code> "Develop Servers" and <code>planning/_planning_tab.tt</code> "Branch Servers") now render externals from <code>build_worktree_list</code> only; the planning tab's branch dropdown/table skip <code>b.external</code> (no Catalyst port) and render externals in their own row.</p>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>3. The registry cannot lose keys to a writer.</strong> <code>_save_worktree_config</code> writes the whole file, so any caller handing it a bare <code>{ base_dir, branches }</code> erased <code>external_repos</code>/<code>update_all_skip</code> for good (the file has no git history). It now re-reads the file and copies back every top-level key the caller did not set.</p>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>4. <code>_run</code> no longer reads a raw <code>target</code> request param.</strong> <code>POST /admin/git/merge</code> uses <code>target=&lt;branch&gt;</code>, so the old fallback resolved a branch name as a host target. Only the stash set by <code>_bind_target</code> binds a target now (the JS already injects the hidden <code>target</code> field into every dashboard form).</p>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>Why.</strong> A lost registry key turned into "the page opened but showed the wrong repository" with no error anywhere, and the Hermes button failed with "worktree git root missing". Failing closed converts both into one explicit red banner: <em>Target not usable: Unknown git target 'x': register it under external_repos…</em></p>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>Files.</strong> <code>Comserv/lib/Comserv/Util/Git.pm</code>, <code>Comserv/lib/Comserv/Controller/Admin/Git.pm</code>, <code>Comserv/root/admin/git/index.tt</code>, <code>Comserv/root/admin/planning/_planning_tab.tt</code>, <code>Comserv/root/config/worktrees.json</code> (local registry data).</p>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>Verify.</strong> <code>perl -Ilib -c</code> on Util/Git.pm, Controller/Admin/Git.pm and Controller/Planning.pm; both templates compile through <code>Template</code>; <code>_save_worktree_config({branches =&gt; …})</code> round-trips with <code>external_repos</code>, <code>update_all_skip</code>, <code>base_dir</code> and <code>port_start</code> intact; <code>resolve_target('openbafang')</code> → repo <code>/home/shanta/dev/OpenBafangTool</code>, Hermes port 9120; a typo target → <code>mode=unknown, repo=UNDEF</code> + error; <code>open_or_start_hermes('openbafang',0)</code> → 9120 listening.</p>
</div>
